  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.25 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is 4 times steeper in x, so overshoot starts there: above a learning rate of 0.5 the x swings grow.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -learning rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)): sd 2 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at batch 64 (the whole simulated data set).",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. Noise comes from a fixed seed: Reset replays the same sequence, and each new run uses the next noise values.",
    "Diverged means the ball has left the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| above 2). The run then stops, and the Loss readout keeps the true value. Otherwise a run is finished after 40 steps.",
    "Arrows are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes next; grey is the full-data step, -learning rate × true slope.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 41). Paths off the bowl are drawn at its edge."
  ]
}